n{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-322731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdd60be2a2b463d1fbf9315ff535bfc3475f1261975508c62b026647a7c851e"} {"question" "q-322731" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-322731" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 & t \\\\ t & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 1-t\\cdot t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdd60be2a2b463d1fbf9315ff535bfc3475f1261975508c62b026647a7c851e"} {"question" "q-333704" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333704" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{t-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdd60be2a2b463d1fbf9315ff535bfc3475f1261975508c62b026647a7c851e"} {"question" "q-333695" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333695" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-352424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -10{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-352424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -10{e}^{3t} & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(5{e}^{3t})\\cdot (2{e}^{3t})-(-{e}^{3t})\\cdot (-10{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =10{e}^{6t}-10{e}^{6t} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-352424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdd60be2a2b463d1fbf9315ff535bfc3475f1261975508c62b026647a7c851e"} {"question" "q-352424" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-352424" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10{e}^{6t}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "10{e}^{6t}"] ["math" "{e}^{6t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/questi