9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdd60be2a2b463d1fbf9315ff535bfc3475f1261975508c62b026647a7c851e"} {"question" "q-333862" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333862" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333862" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333862" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333862" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "nonzero for at least one $t\\in R$"] ["text" "zero for all $t\\in R$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333862" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "nonzero for at least one $t\\in R$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333862" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333862" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "dependent"] ["text" "independent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-333862" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "independent"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-322737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-6{x}_{1}(t)+7{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below. $\\,$ The function $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-322737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(7{e}^{-4t})^{′} \\\\ (2{e}^{-4t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot 7{e}^{-4t}+7\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2\\cdot 7{e}^{-4t}+3\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (-2{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {e}^{t}+7\\cdot (-2{e}^{t}) \\\\ -2\\cdot {e}^{t}+3\\cdot (-2{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-20{e}^{t} \\\\ -8{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {e}^{t}+7\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\ -2\\cdot {e}^{t}+3\\cdot {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,c}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,c) & =|\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(7{e}^{-4t})\\cdot {e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =5{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,c}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-322737" "field" nil "attribute" "