ion" "q-334527" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "a,b"] ["math" "a,c"] ["math" "b,c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-334527" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "a,b"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-334527" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-334527" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{e}^{4t}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "10{e}^{4t}"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "q-334527" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc3ce6898e8f8dab9f9a179a0f257acf4954c5c5c4592c4f8f474f9ac025e99a"} {"question" "e-21179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/b6c879d367709165835c2de7cec94895897be567a9e264222506d4b2a63874e0.png)\nFind the Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ of the above vector-valued functions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/example-21179.json" "file_sha256" "1f92ab7189bf7c5d07d0f900de353b93abe437846380b0ea181fedc9e2b5c790"} {"question" "e-21179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/ebce3767dfd869aa1ebf4bb03b9eb06a7f41dbb4b927a7699982f35d3a43a562.png)\nis defined as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/b07428ffa056ae11d8fef349d666abab80e64bfc1822006f42db42a4b21e596d.png)\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3} & {t}^{3}-5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot 4-({t}^{3}-5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{3}+10 \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/example-21179.json" "file_sha256" "1f92ab7189bf7c5d07d0f900de353b93abe437846380b0ea181fedc9e2b5c790"} {"question" "e-21180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/235991f16a48086f1629a0519b1358db70ee0b26062a651a2a8adc305c682681.png)\nConsider the vector-valued functions $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ above. Given that $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, compute the Wronskian is $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ and determine if the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent on $\\mathbb R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/example-21180.json" "file_sha256" "1c063ad53207ced40b4e1907483031a4f23e4e4fcc9b13be21d1f200893e2dce"} {"question" "e-21180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/51774bbcf79f8ab11069a5d7c955f73cd637f8407aa7a600673de0eaf9adb7ea.png)\nis defined as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/ed9d2deba98ee27ebe8a183cab45f42a92f2d4f49733fa8a8f7c9acdf6185912.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{2t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4{e}^{2t})\\cdot (2{e}^{t})-{e}^{t}\\cdot ({e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =-8{e}^{3t}-{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =-9{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/example-21180.json" "file_sha256" "1c063ad53207ced40b4e1907483031a4f23e4e4fcc9b13be21d1f200893e2dce"} {"question" "e-21181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in\\mathbb R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/74e10d18890ebd27b2339e13ac2bbc9e0821c1dfea752478a90877d0a0059ff6.png) is a solution of the system.\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/f08f12db6684778c1f5734c0731a370663fcfb23cd958dde49e3a2f41cd0e50a.png) is a solution of the system.\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/f5c546b729e994f79408b4344979446aac8aa3820c1798251bc50378f8f7159b.png) is a solution of the system.\n\nAlso, determine which pair of solutions forms a set of fundamental solutions to the system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/example-21181.json" "file_sha256" "834f458f337d12a30652cb5b4f1e143c17e7f118948144d4ea5e5f9d05ad52ea"} {"question" "e-21181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/e61be508e4ad25ed39996a7a924a732290f3c8be7e5e953e01da6ff35c5669f4.png)\nis defined as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/ed9d2deba98ee27ebe8a183cab45f42a92f2d4f49733fa8a8f7c9acdf6185912.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/3ac4270c91e71468086149ed819c644c71d59aea898520acc8e5fbf30205985c.png)\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/7d502d9256ace32899ea72f143f3694db8f0531efebf2dbd0c20dc857bd563b5.png) $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -4t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+1\\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+(-2)\\cdot (-4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-4t+2 \\\\ -4{t}^{2}+8t-4\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/c223cfc85f5a1f23006dd9de1775f898731a2cce2b88d1c27c0ab3f4ee238e66.png) $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2t+1 \\\\ -4t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (2t+1)+1\\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4\\cdot (2t+1)+(-2)\\cdot (-4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/2f5d5dd7f1fe1b1a51df2930d780fc11af9a31f8cac9cc9c38f966eb42bc848d.png) $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (1-2t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1-2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot t+1\\cdot (1-2t) \\\\ -4\\cdot t+(-2)\\cdot (1-2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(b,c) & =|\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & t \\\\ -4t & 1-2t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\cdot (1-2t)-t\\cdot (-4t) \\\\  & =(2t+1)(1-2t)+4{t}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t \\in \\mathbb R.$ Thus, since $\\mathbf b$ and $\\mathbf c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/example-21181.json" "file_sha256" "834f458f337d12a30652cb5b4f1e143c17e7f118948144d4ea5e5f9d05ad52ea"}] "usage" {"q-333862" [] "q-333695" [] "q-322737" [] "e-21181" [] "q-322731" [] "e-21180" [] "e-21179" []} "basis" 2189 "retractions" [[17592186221858 :question/problem "$x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t)\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin (3t) \\\\ \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W(x_{1}, x_{2}) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"identifying-linearly-independent-vector-valued-functions\"></a>"] [17592186221920 :question/problem "$x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ e^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W(x_{1}, x_{2}) =$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186221932 :question/problem "$x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ R$\nConsider the vector-valued functions $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ above. Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below. The Wronskian is $W(x_{1}, x_{2}) =$\n{{field-1}},\n\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the set ${x_{1}(t), x_{2}(t)}$ is linearly\n{{field-3}}\non $R$."] [17592186221859 :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-6x_{1}(t) + 7x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-2x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t)$.\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t ∈ R$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. The function $a(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}7e^{-4t} \\\\ 2e^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n{{field-1}}\na solution of the system.\n\nThe function $b(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ -2e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n{{field-2}}\na solution of the system.\n\nThe function $c(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n{{field-3}}\na solution of the system."] [17592186221087 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}t^{3} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ of the above vector-valued functions."] [17592186221087 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nis defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}x_{11}(t) & x_{12}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t) & x_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(x_{1}, x_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}t^{3} & t^{3} - 5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= t^{3} \\cdot 4 - (t^{3} - 5) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 4t^{3} - 2t^{3} + 10 \\\\\n&= 2t^{3} + 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221088 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4e^{2t} \\\\ e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vector-valued functions $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ above. Given that $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, compute the Wronskian is $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ and determine if the set $\\left\\{\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent on $\\mathbb R$."] [17592186221088 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nis defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}x_{11}(t) & x_{12}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t) & x_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(x_{1}, x_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}-4e^{2t} & e^{t} \\\\ e^{2t} & 2e^{t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (-4e^{2t}) \\cdot (2e^{t}) - e^{t} \\cdot (e^{2t}) \\\\\n&=-8e^{3t} - e^{3t} \\\\\n&=-9e^{3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in \\mathbb R$.\n\nTherefore, the set\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nis linearly independent on $\\mathbb R$."] [17592186221089 :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 2x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-4x_{1}(t) - 2x_{2}(t)$.\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in\\mathbb R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function $\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the system.\n2. The function $\\begin{bmatrix}2t + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the system.\n3. The function $\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the system.\n\nAlso, determine which pair of solutions forms a set of fundamental solutions to the system."] [17592186221089 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nis defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}x_{11}(t) & x_{12}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t) & x_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}(t^{2} + 1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot (t^{2} + 1) + 1 \\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4 \\cdot (t^{2} + 1) + (-2) \\cdot (-4t)\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2t^{2} - 4t + 2 \\\\ -4t^{2} + 8t - 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot$\n\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}2t + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}(2t + 1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2t + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot (2t + 1) + 1 \\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4 \\cdot (2t + 1) + (-2) \\cdot (-4t)\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}✓$\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}(t)^{′} \\\\ (1 - 2t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot t + 1 \\cdot (1 - 2t) \\\\ -4 \\cdot t + (-2) \\cdot (1 - 2t)\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}✓$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions $\\big\\{\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}$. Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(b, c) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2t + 1 & t \\\\ -4t & 1 - 2t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (2t + 1) \\cdot (1 - 2t) - t \\cdot (-4t) \\\\\n&= (2t + 1)(1 - 2t) + 4t^{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t \\in \\mathbb R$. Thus, since $\\mathbf b$ and $\\mathbf c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\big\\{\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220062 17592186220063 17592186220065 17592186220066 17592186220068 17592186220069 17592186220562 17592186220563 17592186220564 17592186220565 17592186220567 17592186220568 17592186220570 17592186220571] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220060 :answer-field/id] [17592186220060 :answer-field/key] [17592186220060 :answer-field/type] [17592186220061 :answer-field/id] [17592186220061 :answer-field/key] [17592186220061 :answer-field/type] [17592186220064 :answer-field/id] [17592186220064 :answer-field/key] [17592186220064 :answer-field/type] [17592186220067 :answer-field/id] [17592186220067 :answer-field/key] [17592186220067 :answer-field/type] [17592186220466 :answer-field/id] [17592186220466 :answer-field/key] [17592186220466 :answer-field/type] [17592186220561 :answer-field/id] [17592186220561 :answer-field/key] [17592186220561 :answer-field/type] [17592186220566 :answer-field/id] [17592186220566 :answer-field/key] [17592186220566 :answer-field